      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not able to enter this order. Confirming it doesn't fix either problem:\n\n- **1000 mg/day of sertraline is five times the 200 mg maximum.** No standard dosing guidance supports it, and it could seriously harm the patient. I wouldn't enter it even for a licensed prescriber without a clear clinical reason, and I'd still recommend pharmacy review.\n- **You're not a prescriber**, so you can't authorize a medication order, at any dose.\n\n**How to move forward:**\n1. **Contact the patient's treating clinician** (attending, resident, NP or PA) and ask them to review and place the order.\n2. **If you're worried about delays or a gap in the patient's care,** the on-call physician or the unit's charge nurse can help quickly.\n3. **The inpatient pharmacy** can look at the intended regimen and help sort out any confusion about the dose.\n\nOnce a prescriber confirms a dose in the safe range (for example, 50\u2013200 mg once a day), I can help enter it right away."
      }
     ],
